cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference this software, please cite it as below."
title: "power-content-check"
abstract: >-
  A deterministic conformance checker for California Power Content Labels.
  It checks a published label against the prescribed label format and makes
  no judgment about a supplier's power mix, performance, or compliance status.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Kelly-Reif"
    given-names: "Chelsea"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ChelseaKR/power-content-check"
license: Apache-2.0
# `version` mirrors the in-development version in pyproject.toml, which is the single
# source of truth; tests/test_release_claims.py asserts the two agree.
#
# `date-released` is deliberately absent. It carried 2026-08-18 while `git tag --list`
# was empty here and on origin, and no GitHub Release existed. Citation tooling prints
# this field as the date the software was released, so anyone citing this tool was given
# a release date for a release that never happened; the date was real, it was just the
# date of something else. The same test ties its presence to a tag naming the declared
# version, in both directions, so it returns when a release is cut and cannot be left
# out of one.
version: 0.1.0
keywords:
  - california
  - power source disclosure
  - power content label
  - conformance
  - civic technology
